# Line endings for the native sources under compiler/.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS SCOPED TO ONE DIRECTORY.
#
# The tree is checked out on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, and the same tree
# is built and measured from WSL over /mnt/c. That combination has already cost
# a real bug once: reading the working copy from the Linux side made every text
# file look modified, so a "is the tree dirty?" flag saturated to true and
# carried no information.
#
# The fix for compiler/ is to take the ambiguity away for the files that get
# compiled and hashed: they are LF on disk everywhere, so a digest taken on one
# side matches a digest taken on the other.
#
# It is deliberately NOT `* text=auto`. A repository-wide rule would renormalise
# every tracked file on the next checkout, which moves the bytes of the shipped
# bundles — the one thing the release-time invariants exist to keep still.
compiler/** text eol=lf

# ── AND THE SECOND DIRECTORY, FOR A DIFFERENT REASON ─────────────────────────
#
# `scripts/**/*.mjs` is here because a CRLF checkout does not merely look
# different to the Linux side — it stops a test suite from running at all, and
# does so SILENTLY.
#
# The mechanism, reduced to its minimum: a module whose first line is a shebang
# terminated by CRLF, and which also contains an `import`, cannot be loaded by
# vitest. It fails with `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token` attributed to
# a line in the IMPORTING file — a comment line, in the case that produced this
# entry — so the report points at the wrong file and at a line that cannot be
# the cause. A shebang with LF is fine; CRLF with no shebang is fine; CRLF with
# no import is fine. It takes all three.
#
# The consequence was that `scripts/check-doc-drift.test.mjs` contributed 42
# passing tests on every Linux runner and ZERO on the author's machine, where
# the suite was recorded as a collection error and read as "a pre-existing
# vitest quirk". Two separate sessions diagnosed it as an encoding or extension
# problem, because converting the TEST file changes nothing: the CRLF that
# matters is in the module under test.
#
# The blobs are already LF (verified: 0 CR bytes in every tracked file under
# `scripts/`), so this line moves no committed byte. It changes only what a
# Windows checkout materialises, which is precisely the divergence that hid the
# failure. Scoped to `*.mjs` rather than `scripts/**` on purpose: the egress
# fixtures under `scripts/fixtures/` are compared as bytes by the A2 test, and
# they are not part of the defect class.
scripts/**/*.mjs text eol=lf
